const components=[['Transformer','Changes AC voltage and, when designed for it, provides galvanic isolation. Conventional low-frequency and high-frequency SMPS transformers occupy different circuit stages.'],['Diodes / bridge','Steer current to create a unidirectional output. Silicon and Schottky devices differ in forward loss, reverse-voltage capability and leakage; use operating-point data.'],['Capacitor','Stores energy across the DC bus. Capacitance, voltage rating, ESR, ripple-current rating, temperature and lifetime all matter.'],['Inductor','Stores magnetic energy and limits current change in filters or switching stages. Saturation and current ratings must be checked.'],['Resistors','Provide loads, sensing, feedback or discharge paths. Resistance and power dissipation are separate ratings.'],['Regulator / control IC','Adjusts energy transfer or pass-element conduction to control voltage or current. It needs headroom, stable feedback and appropriate protection.'],['Heat sink','Transfers device losses to the environment. A low calculated loss does not establish a safe junction temperature.'],['Fuse and protection','Help manage abnormal conditions. Fuses, surge suppression, protective earth, insulation and spacing perform different functions.']];
const faq=[['What is an AC to DC converter?','A circuit or power-supply assembly that accepts alternating current and provides a DC output. A rectifier establishes one-way current; filtering, regulation, isolation and protection may be additional stages.'],['How do you convert AC voltage to DC?','Select a suitable rectifier, account for its conducting diode path and output waveform, then consider filtering and regulation for the load. Use a correctly rated isolated supply for practical mains-powered equipment.'],['What is the difference between a rectifier and a complete converter?','A rectifier can produce pulsating DC. A complete regulated converter may also provide isolation, energy storage, voltage or current control and protection. The words are sometimes used loosely; inspect the actual stages.'],['Which rectifier is best?','There is no universal best topology. Source winding, load power, available headroom, ripple, isolation and thermal constraints determine the choice. A bridge is common for single-phase supplies but is not loss-free.'],['How do I calculate DC output voltage?','First decide whether you need an unfiltered waveform average, a filtered estimate or a regulated setpoint. RMS, peak, average and ripple valley answer different questions. The calculator separates these models.'],['How do you convert 220 V AC to 12 V DC?','Use an appropriately rated, isolated AC-to-DC supply specified for the input and load. Rectifying 220 V RMS directly produces a crest near 311 V before losses, not 12 V. This site does not provide exposed-mains construction instructions.'],['What does the filter capacitor do?','It supplies load current between charging peaks, reducing voltage variation. A heavier load or smaller capacitance generally increases ripple. It does not create regulated voltage or guarantee a safe output.'],['What is ripple voltage?','Residual variation around the DC component. Peak-to-peak ripple is highest minus lowest voltage; RMS ripple factor is AC-component RMS divided by average DC. These are different measures.'],['How do half-wave and full-wave rectification differ?','Half-wave uses one half-cycle and has pulse frequency f. Full-wave uses both halves and has pulse frequency 2f for a single-phase sine input. Their complete-cycle averages and filter discharge intervals differ.'],['What affects output voltage?','Input waveform and RMS convention, rectifier topology, diode loss, source impedance, filter size, load, line variation and regulator behavior. A voltage label alone does not establish available current.'],['How are RMS and peak voltage related?','For a sine wave, Vpeak = √2 Vrms. This relationship does not apply unchanged to square waves or arbitrary distorted signals. Peak-to-peak sine voltage is twice its peak amplitude.'],['How efficient is AC-to-DC conversion?','It depends on the complete architecture and operating point. Measure real input watts and output watts for whole-supply efficiency. A diode-loss estimate or regulator-stage efficiency is not the same quantity.']];
